What the software writes into its output

    Every PDF it writes names it as the Producer, in the document information
    and in the XMP metadata, as "Marmalade Open Press <version>
    (https://marmalade.family)". A page drawn by the software's own render
    step also names it as the Creator, as "Marmalade Open Press <version>,
    with Chromium". Every TIFF page names it in the Software tag, next to
    Ghostscript. This is the ordinary way a file records the program that
    made it. Nothing is sent anywhere: the software makes no network request
    of its own, apart from the page you ask it to open.

Third-party software

    The package depends on, and does not include, these libraries, each under
    its own licence:

    - pdf-lib, MIT License
    - Playwright, Apache License 2.0, which downloads Chromium separately
    - sharp, Apache License 2.0, with libvips under the LGPL 3.0 or later

    The PDF/X-1a, PDF/X-3 and TIFF formats use Ghostscript, if it is
    installed on the machine. Ghostscript is a separate program under the
    GNU Affero General Public License. It is not part of this package and is
    not distributed with it.

    Without --profile, the software uses the CMYK profile that libvips
    carries as a stand-in, "Chemical proof", and embeds it in the PDF as the
    output intent; the example PDFs in examples/ carry it. Its own copyright
    tag reads: "Created by Graeme W. Gill. Released into the public domain.
    No Warranty, Use at your own risk."
